.TH CCOL_FIXED_WIDTH_DATA_TYPE_SIZE 3 "2026-09-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size \- byte size of the C type a ccol_data_type names
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <common.h>

size_t ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size(ccol_data_type type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns sizeof() of the C type the enumerator stands for, for every
ccol_data_type that names one specific C type. ccol_string,
ccol_other_types, and any unrecognized value return 0: a key or value of
those types legitimately arrives at any size, so there is no one width
to report.

This is the width a key_pair must carry for a fixed-width key type, and
what every chmap/cbmap entry point taking one requires of it. Code
building a cmap_pair by hand can call this to size one correctly.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
type   Data type enumerator to size
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns sizeof() of the corresponding C type
Returns 0 if the type has no fixed width (ccol_string, ccol_other_types,
or any unrecognized value)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Defined as a static inline function in common.h; no library symbol is
exported for it
The returned width is this platform's own sizeof(), so it varies with
the ABI for long, pointer, and long double
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
size_t width = ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size(ccol_int);
int key = 42;
cmap_pair kp = { .ptr = &key, .size = width };
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_insert_elem (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3),
.BR chmap_insert_elem (3)
